course: dictionary
lesson: 907
title: One word, two meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A word can name a thing, and it can name a plant too. Blackberry is a noun: a blackberry is the small dark fruit, and the blackberry is also the bush it grows on. Context tells you which one is meant. «Pick blackberries only when a grown-up has identified them» means the fruit, because you pick fruit, not a bush.
explain: Look at the words near the word. «Blackberry picking» and «blackberry jam» are about the fruit. Words placed before another noun, like blackberry in «blackberry and apple crumble», describe the thing that comes after them. Ask yourself: what is being picked, cooked or eaten?
explain: Nouns can also change to show more than one: one blackberry, many blackberries. Notice that the y changes to ies. Blackberrying is a verb built from the noun, and in the UK it means going out to pick blackberries.
fact: A blackberry is a small soft dark purple-black fruit made of many tiny round parts.
fact: Blackberries grow on a thorny bush called a bramble.
fact: «Blackberry picking» means gathering the fruit, and blackberrying is the UK word for doing it.
fact: Botanically, bananas, grapes and tomatoes are berries, but blackberries are not true berries, even though we call them that.
fact: In late summer, blackberries grow wild in hedges and woods, and birds and animals eat them and spread the seeds.
exercise: «Pick blackberries only when a grown-up has identified them.» What does blackberries mean here? | *The small dark fruit | A thorny bush with no fruit | A bird that eats berries | A jar of jam
exercise: Which sentence uses blackberry to mean the bush? | *The blackberry grew over the fence, full of thorns | Dad ate three blackberries | The jam was sweet and dark | Grandma made a blackberry crumble
exercise: What is a bramble? | A kind of jam | A small purple fruit | *A thorny bush that blackberries grow on | A stone wall in a field
test: Which word means more than one blackberry? | blackberry | *blackberries | blackberrys | blackberrying
test: Why must you wash wild blackberries before eating them? | Because they are too big to eat | Because they grow only in the desert | *Because some look alike and some are poisonous | Because washing makes them sweeter
exam: Which is the best reason an adult must check wild blackberries before anyone picks them? | Some berries look alike and some are poisonous | Blackberries are always too sour to eat | *Some berries look alike and some are poisonous, so only safe ones should be picked | Blackberries only grow in the desert
